{"questions":[{"prompt":"You are selecting a Monstera deliciosa cutting for water propagation. The plant has both mature and juvenile stems. Which described cutting is most likely to root successfully?","expectedAnswer":"A section with two firm green leaves, a visible node, and a short aerial root","choices":["A section with two firm green leaves, a visible node, and a short aerial root","A tip with one unfurling leaf but no node below the cut point","A lower leafless section with a visible node but no remaining leaves","A section with three healthy leaves and a node, but the base is soft and dark brown"],"explanation":"Successful Monstera cuttings need at least one node (the site of root emergence) and healthy foliage for photosynthesis. An existing aerial root accelerates rooting. No node means no root initiation, leafless sections lack energy production, and a soft dark-brown base indicates rot.","topic":"Choosing viable stem material","objective":"Evaluate stem characteristics to select a cutting most likely to root","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"},{"prompt":"Two weeks ago you placed Tradescantia cuttings in water inside a sealed clear plastic bag on a bright windowsill. The stems now show white fuzz at the waterline, lower leaves are mushy, and no roots have formed. What is the most likely primary cause?","expectedAnswer":"Sealed bag eliminated air exchange, creating stagnant humidity that promoted fungal growth and rot","choices":["Sealed bag eliminated air exchange, creating stagnant humidity that promoted fungal growth and rot","Too much light through the bag caused heat damage that prevented root hormone activation","Tradescantia requires soil rather than water for propagation to succeed","The cuttings needed rooting hormone powder to initiate root growth in water"],"explanation":"Tradescantia roots readily in plain water, so rooting hormone and soil are not required. While bright light can cause heat in a sealed bag, the white fuzz is characteristic mold from stagnant, overly humid conditions with no air circulation. Ventilation or an open container would prevent this.","topic":"Managing rooting moisture, humidity and light","objective":"Diagnose environmental problems during the rooting phase based on observable symptoms","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"},{"prompt":"You water-propagated Philodendron cuttings at different times. A: 0.5 cm nubs just emerging. B: 3 cm white roots with secondary branching. C: 12 cm tangled translucent roots, tips browning. D: no roots after four weeks, soft stem base. Which group is ideal for potting into soil?","expectedAnswer":"Group B, because 2–4 cm branching roots transplant and adapt to soil well","choices":["Group A, because very short roots adapt to soil fastest","Group B, because 2–4 cm branching roots transplant and adapt to soil well","Group C, because longer roots anchor the cutting more securely in soil","Group D, because it should move to soil since water propagation is not working"],"explanation":"Roots 2–4 cm long with branching are established enough to take up water in soil yet still adaptable. Group A's nubs are too fragile and may not survive transition. Group C's over-developed water roots often struggle to function in soil. Group D shows likely rot and should be discarded, not potted.","topic":"Deciding when to pot rooted cuttings","objective":"Assess root development to determine the optimal timing for transplanting into soil","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"}]}
```

