{"solutions":[
  {
    "index":0,
    "outcome":"resolved",
    "answer":"The cutting most likely to root is a stem section that includes at least one node with an axillary bud (and ideally two nodes), taken from healthy, pest-/disease-free material. Per the UMN source, Monstera deliciosa propagation *requires* a node with an axillary bud; a detached leaf-and-petiole piece lacking stem node tissue will not produce a new plant. The cutting should be 3–6 inches long (Iowa State guideline), with lower leaves removed and at least one node submerged. Whether the stem is mature or juvenile matters less than the presence of a viable node with bud tissue.",
    "limitations":"The prompt references described cuttings that appear to be in unseen answer choices; the principle applied is that a node with axillary bud is the non-negotiable requirement per the UMN source.",
    "assumptionsRequired":[]
  },
  {
    "index":1,
    "outcome":"resolved",
    "answer":"The most likely primary cause is the sealed plastic bag eliminating air exchange, creating a stagnant, excessively humid environment that promoted fungal/bacterial rot. The Iowa State source specifies that humidity covers must be loose and allow air exchange. A sealed bag traps moisture and heat (especially on a bright windowsill), encouraging pathogen growth (the white fuzz is likely mold/fungus) and tissue decay (mushy leaves, soft stems). Bright light through the sealed cover also intensifies heat stress. Opening or loosening the cover and reducing direct light exposure would have been needed.",
    "limitations":"",
    "assumptionsRequired":[]
  },
  {
    "index":2,
    "outcome":"resolved",
    "answer":"Group B (3 cm white roots with secondary branching) is ideal for potting into soil. The Illinois Extension guide recommends potting when roots are about one inch long (~2.5 cm); 3 cm is close to that benchmark, and the white color and secondary branching indicate healthy, actively growing roots. The UMN source advises returning cuttings with absent or sparse roots to the medium, ruling out A (0.5 cm nubs, too sparse) and D (no roots, soft base indicating rot/failure). Group C (12 cm tangled roots with browning tips) has been left in water too long and is showing decline; water-adapted roots also transition to soil less readily when overgrown.",
    "limitations":"",
    "assumptionsRequired":[]
  }
]}
