{"questions":[{"prompt":"A form redisplays after failed submission. Invalid fields have red borders. A banner at the top reads \"There are errors on this page.\" No text identifies which fields have errors or what is wrong. Which assessment of SC 3.3.1 compliance is correct?","expectedAnswer":"It fails because the error text does not identify the affected items or describe what is wrong","choices":["It fails because the error text does not identify the affected items or describe what is wrong","It passes because the banner provides a text notification and the red borders show which fields","It passes because the page was redisplayed with visual error indicators present","It fails only because color is used; adding a red icon beside each field would fix it"],"explanation":"SC 3.3.1 requires text that identifies the affected item and describes what is wrong. A generic banner without field-specific messages fails this. Red borders are color alone and cannot supply the required text description. An icon without descriptive text would not resolve the issue either.","topic":"identifying form errors","skillID":"","objectiveID":"","objective":"Evaluate whether an error-reporting implementation meets SC 3.3.1 requirements","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"},{"prompt":"A date-of-birth input uses aria-label=\"Date of birth\" and placeholder=\"MM/DD/YYYY\". No visible text label appears near the field. Which SC 3.3.2 assessment is correct?","expectedAnswer":"It fails because aria-label alone does not provide a visible label or instruction to non-AT users","choices":["It fails because aria-label alone does not provide a visible label or instruction to non-AT users","It passes because aria-label supplies the accessible name that SC 3.3.2 requires","It passes because the placeholder text serves as both a visible label and format instruction","It fails only because the placeholder disappears on input; a persistent placeholder would pass"],"explanation":"SC 3.3.2 requires labels or instructions presented to all users. aria-label provides a programmatic name exposed only through assistive technology, not a visible cue. The placeholder shows a date format but does not identify the field's purpose as date of birth and disappears on input.","topic":"visible form labels","skillID":"","objectiveID":"","objective":"Distinguish visible labeling from programmatic-only naming under SC 3.3.2","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"},{"prompt":"A form shows visible \"Email\" text inside a span element, followed by an input with id=\"email\". The span has no for attribute and no ARIA attributes. Which statement about this field's accessibility is accurate?","expectedAnswer":"It provides a visible label but lacks a programmatic association for assistive technology","choices":["It provides a visible label but lacks a programmatic association for assistive technology","It meets both visible labeling and programmatic association requirements","It fails both visible labeling and programmatic association requirements","It meets programmatic association because the label text is adjacent to the input"],"explanation":"The visible \"Email\" text satisfies the need for a visible cue (SC 3.3.2). However, a span element without a for attribute, label wrapping, or ARIA linkage creates no programmatic association. Assistive technology cannot determine that \"Email\" labels this input; proximity alone is insufficient.","topic":"explicit label associations","skillID":"","objectiveID":"","objective":"Distinguish visible labeling from programmatic label association in HTML forms","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"}]}
