{"solutions":[
  {
    "index":0,
    "outcome":"resolved",
    "answer":"The <label> element has no 'for' attribute linking it to the input's id=\"email\". Without an explicit association (for=\"email\") or implicit association (wrapping the input inside the <label>), the label is not programmatically connected to the control. Speech-input tools match visible label text to the associated control; because no association exists, saying \"click Email\" cannot move focus to the field. The aria-label provides a programmatic accessible name to assistive technology but does not create the label-to-control association that speech-input software needs via the <label> element.",
    "limitations":"",
    "assumptionsRequired":[]
  },
  {
    "index":1,
    "outcome":"resolved",
    "answer":"The individual invalid fields are identified only by color (red border) with no accompanying text description of each error. Per SC 3.3.1, input errors that are automatically detected must be identified in text that describes the item in error and what is wrong. The generic banner says errors exist but does not identify which fields are affected, and the red border alone communicates the per-field error through color only. A color-blind user cannot perceive the red borders, leaving them with no way to determine which fields need correction. Adding inline text messages for each erroneous field would resolve the barrier.",
    "limitations":"",
    "assumptionsRequired":[]
  },
  {
    "index":2,
    "outcome":"resolved",
    "answer":"The required date format (MM/DD/YYYY) is conveyed only through the placeholder attribute, which disappears as soon as the user begins typing. This means the format instruction is not persistently visible. Per SC 3.3.2, labels or instructions should communicate expected input formats. Placeholder text is an unreliable substitute for a visible, persistent instruction because users lose the cue mid-entry. The format instruction should be provided as permanent visible text (e.g., as part of the label or as helper text adjacent to the field) so users can reference it while typing.",
    "limitations":"",
    "assumptionsRequired":[]
  }
]}
